{
  "teamId": 118,
  "year": 2017,
  "narrative": "The 2017 Kansas City Royals began the season as defending American League contenders, but the calendar turned out to be as uneven as the roster itself. April was a disaster — a 7-16 record that buried them before May arrived — and the team's bullpen, led by the usually reliable Kelvin Herrera, proved to be as much a liability as an asset. Herrera surrendered some of the most gut-wrenching home runs of the year, and early collapses against Houston, Minnesota, and Cleveland set a punishing tone.\n\nBut the Royals refused to fold. May brought a steadying hand, and June and July were the best baseball the team played all year. Mike Moustakas was on a historic home run pace, Whit Merrifield delivered clutch extra-base hits seemingly every week, and Salvador Perez provided thunder with a grand slam and walk-off moments. The team went 33-19 over those two months, briefly rekindling playoff hopes and reminding Kauffman Stadium why this core had won it all in 2015.\n\nAugust shattered those dreams. A 10-18 collapse, fueled by bullpen disasters — Yadier Molina's grand slam off Peter Moylan, a Matt Joyce three-run double off Mike Minor, back-to-back Oakland home runs — erased all the goodwill the summer had built. Eric Hosmer delivered one of the season's most electric moments with a three-run walk-off homer on August 23rd, but even that couldn't stop the bleeding. The playoff window slammed shut, and the Royals finished below .500 at 80-82.\n\nSeptember was a quiet, bittersweet farewell. With Lorenzo Cain, Eric Hosmer, Mike Moustakas, and others all headed for free agency, every game carried the weight of an ending. Jorge Bonifacio showed flashes of future promise, Paulo Orlando homered in one of his final Royals appearances, and the Kelvin Herrera bullpen woes continued right to the finish. The 2017 Royals were a team caught between its championship past and an uncertain future — talented enough to flirt with contention, but too flawed to recapture October magic.",
